{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01H7771f4dxLDZbWV1zAGYaF","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tsource_url: \"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/overview/components/\"\n3\tfetched_at: \"2026-08-23T22:30:00-0400\"\n4\tauthority: \"Kubernetes project (kubernetes.io/docs)\"\n5\tobjectives_covered: [\"D1 Core Concepts\", \"D1 Administration\"]\n6\tconcepts_covered: [\"control-plane-components\", \"node-components\", \"addons\"]\n7\t---\n8\t# Kubernetes Components (kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/overview/components/)\n9\t\n10\tA Kubernetes cluster consists of a control plane and one or more worker nodes.\n11\t\n12\t## Control plane components — manage the overall state of the cluster\n13\t- **kube-apiserver** — The core component server that exposes the Kubernetes HTTP API.\n14\t- **etcd** — Consistent and highly-available key value store for all API server data.\n15\t- **kube-scheduler** — Looks for Pods not yet bound to a node, and assigns each Pod to a suitable node.\n16\t- **kube-controller-manager** — Runs controllers to implement Kubernetes API behavior.\n17\t- **cloud-controller-manager** (optional) — Integrates with underlying cloud provider(s).\n18\t\n19\t## Node components — run on every node, maintaining running pods and providing the Kubernetes runtime environment\n20\t- **kubelet** — Ensures that Pods are running, including their containers.\n21\t- **kube-proxy** (optional) — Maintains network rules on nodes to implement Services.\n22\t- **Container runtime** — Software responsible for running containers.\n23\t\n24\t## Addons — extend the functionality of Kubernetes\n25\t- **DNS** — For cluster-wide DNS resolution.\n26\t- **Web UI (Dashboard)** — For cluster management via a web interface.\n27\t- **Container Resource Monitoring** — For collecting and storing container metrics.\n28\t- **Cluster-level Logging** — For saving container logs to a central log store.\n29\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ccf3f627-6476-4f00-aaf5-561fbd0f1a2c","uuid":"4b6769f3-eb5e-49f1-8373-77894050e7b1","timestamp":"2026-08-24T07:03:43.161Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\sources\\k8s-docs-components-2026-08-23.md","content":"---\nsource_url: \"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/overview/components/\"\nfetched_at: \"2026-08-23T22:30:00-0400\"\nauthority: \"Kubernetes project (kubernetes.io/docs)\"\nobjectives_covered: [\"D1 Core Concepts\", \"D1 Administration\"]\nconcepts_covered: [\"control-plane-components\", \"node-components\", \"addons\"]\n---\n# Kubernetes Components (kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/overview/components/)\n\nA Kubernetes cluster consists of a control plane and one or more worker nodes.\n\n## Control plane components — manage the overall state of the cluster\n- **kube-apiserver** — The core component server that exposes the Kubernetes HTTP API.\n- **etcd** — Consistent and highly-available key value store for all API server data.\n- **kube-scheduler** — Looks for Pods not yet bound to a node, and assigns each Pod to a suitable node.\n- **kube-controller-manager** — Runs controllers to implement Kubernetes API behavior.\n- **cloud-controller-manager** (optional) — Integrates with underlying cloud provider(s).\n\n## Node components — run on every node, maintaining running pods and providing the Kubernetes runtime environment\n- **kubelet** — Ensures that Pods are running, including their containers.\n- **kube-proxy** (optional) — Maintains network rules on nodes to implement Services.\n- **Container runtime** — Software responsible for running containers.\n\n## Addons — extend the functionality of Kubernetes\n- **DNS** — For cluster-wide DNS resolution.\n- **Web UI (Dashboard)** — For cluster management via a web interface.\n- **Container Resource Monitoring** — For collecting and storing container metrics.\n- **Cluster-level Logging** — For saving container logs to a central log store.\n","numLines":29,"startLine":1,"totalLines":29}}}
