{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011NT7yaJu43UuEf2LoTe9Cj","type":"tool_result","content":"# Communication between Nodes and the Control Plane - Summary\n\nBased on the Kubernetes documentation page, here are the key points:\n\n## Node to Control Plane\n\nKubernetes uses a **\"hub-and-spoke\" API pattern** where:\n- All API usage from nodes and pods terminates at the API server\n- Other control plane components are NOT designed to expose remote services\n- The API server listens on a secure HTTPS port (typically 443)\n- Nodes are provisioned with the public root certificate to connect securely\n- Pods use service accounts, with Kubernetes automatically injecting the certificate and bearer token\n\nThe default mode is **secured by default** and can run over untrusted/public networks.\n\n## Control Plane to Node\n\nThere are two primary communication paths from the API server to nodes:\n\n### 1. API Server to Kubelet\nUsed for:\n- Fetching pod logs\n- Attaching to running pods\n- Port-forwarding functionality\n\n**Security concern**: By default, the API server does NOT verify the kubelet's serving certificate, making it unsafe over untrusted networks. Recommendations include:\n- Use `--kubelet-certificate-authority` flag\n- Use SSH tunneling if needed\n- Enable kubelet authentication/authorization\n\n### 2. API Server to Nodes, Pods, and Services\n- Default to **plain HTTP** (unencrypted and unauthenticated)\n- Can use HTTPS by prefixing `https:` but doesn't validate certificates or provide client credentials\n- **NOT safe** for untrusted or public networks\n\n## Alternative Solutions\n\n**SSH Tunnels**: Currently deprecated; provides SSH tunneling for control plane-to-node traffic.\n\n**Konnectivity Service** (Beta since v1.18): Replacement for SSH tunnels using TCP-level proxy with:\n- Konnectivity server in control plane network\n- Konnectivity agents in nodes network\n- Agents initiate and maintain connections to the server"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ccf3f627-6476-4f00-aaf5-561fbd0f1a2c","uuid":"40ed619f-63d9-4728-852d-c4e4c788f8ff","timestamp":"2026-08-24T07:04:00.364Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":493919,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Communication between Nodes and the Control Plane - Summary\n\nBased on the Kubernetes documentation page, here are the key points:\n\n## Node to Control Plane\n\nKubernetes uses a **\"hub-and-spoke\" API pattern** where:\n- All API usage from nodes and pods terminates at the API server\n- Other control plane components are NOT designed to expose remote services\n- The API server listens on a secure HTTPS port (typically 443)\n- Nodes are provisioned with the public root certificate to connect securely\n- Pods use service accounts, with Kubernetes automatically injecting the certificate and bearer token\n\nThe default mode is **secured by default** and can run over untrusted/public networks.\n\n## Control Plane to Node\n\nThere are two primary communication paths from the API server to nodes:\n\n### 1. API Server to Kubelet\nUsed for:\n- Fetching pod logs\n- Attaching to running pods\n- Port-forwarding functionality\n\n**Security concern**: By default, the API server does NOT verify the kubelet's serving certificate, making it unsafe over untrusted networks. Recommendations include:\n- Use `--kubelet-certificate-authority` flag\n- Use SSH tunneling if needed\n- Enable kubelet authentication/authorization\n\n### 2. API Server to Nodes, Pods, and Services\n- Default to **plain HTTP** (unencrypted and unauthenticated)\n- Can use HTTPS by prefixing `https:` but doesn't validate certificates or provide client credentials\n- **NOT safe** for untrusted or public networks\n\n## Alternative Solutions\n\n**SSH Tunnels**: Currently deprecated; provides SSH tunneling for control plane-to-node traffic.\n\n**Konnectivity Service** (Beta since v1.18): Replacement for SSH tunnels using TCP-level proxy with:\n- Konnectivity server in control plane network\n- Konnectivity agents in nodes network\n- Agents initiate and maintain connections to the server","durationMs":7903,"url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/architecture/control-plane-node-communication/"}}
