{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017d7998PCHdmjdJDpYvMgcL","type":"tool_result","content":"# etcd Definition and Security Requirements\n\n**Introductory Definition:**\n\"etcd is a strongly consistent, distributed key-value store that serves as Kubernetes' primary datastore for all cluster state and critical configuration.\"\n\n**Securing etcd clusters section:**\n\n\"Access to etcd is equivalent to root permission in the cluster so ideally only the API server should have access to it.\"\n\nThe documentation emphasizes restricting permissions: \"it is recommended to grant permission to only those nodes that require access to etcd clusters.\"\n\nSecurity implementation involves establishing encrypted channels through PKI, configuring TLS for both peer communication (using `peer.key` and `peer.cert`) and client communication (using `client.key` and `client.cert`).\n\nFor access restriction, the guidance states: \"restrict the access of the etcd cluster to only the Kubernetes API servers using TLS authentication,\" with client certificate verification enabled via the `--client-cert-auth` flag and CA specification through `--trusted-ca-file`."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ccf3f627-6476-4f00-aaf5-561fbd0f1a2c","uuid":"85dcb557-7100-4e11-8e79-654b66791467","timestamp":"2026-08-24T07:05:10.413Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":19964,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# etcd Definition and Security Requirements\n\n**Introductory Definition:**\n\"etcd is a strongly consistent, distributed key-value store that serves as Kubernetes' primary datastore for all cluster state and critical configuration.\"\n\n**Securing etcd clusters section:**\n\n\"Access to etcd is equivalent to root permission in the cluster so ideally only the API server should have access to it.\"\n\nThe documentation emphasizes restricting permissions: \"it is recommended to grant permission to only those nodes that require access to etcd clusters.\"\n\nSecurity implementation involves establishing encrypted channels through PKI, configuring TLS for both peer communication (using `peer.key` and `peer.cert`) and client communication (using `client.key` and `client.cert`).\n\nFor access restriction, the guidance states: \"restrict the access of the etcd cluster to only the Kubernetes API servers using TLS authentication,\" with client certificate verification enabled via the `--client-cert-auth` flag and CA specification through `--trusted-ca-file`.","durationMs":3806,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/website/main/content/en/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/configure-upgrade-etcd.md"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JRd3oXuZcZX66fuy2aLQMg","type":"tool_result","content":"# DNS Cluster Addon Overview\n\nThe page's introduction section states: \"DNS is a built-in Kubernetes service launched automatically using the _addon manager_ [cluster add-on].\"\n\nHowever, the document does not explicitly state whether the DNS addon is enabled by default in supported environments. It assumes CoreDNS is already running, noting in the prerequisites: \"Your cluster must be running the CoreDNS add-on.\"\n\nRegarding CoreDNS as the default DNS provider, the page explains that CoreDNS serves as the cluster's DNS implementation, describing it as \"a general-purpose authoritative DNS server that can serve as cluster DNS.\" The document treats CoreDNS as the standard implementation being configured rather than explicitly declaring it the default."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ccf3f627-6476-4f00-aaf5-561fbd0f1a2c","uuid":"62dc06f8-86fc-42ee-a16d-5a2063b71141","timestamp":"2026-08-24T07:05:12.473Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":7584,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# DNS Cluster Addon Overview\n\nThe page's introduction section states: \"DNS is a built-in Kubernetes service launched automatically using the _addon manager_ [cluster add-on].\"\n\nHowever, the document does not explicitly state whether the DNS addon is enabled by default in supported environments. It assumes CoreDNS is already running, noting in the prerequisites: \"Your cluster must be running the CoreDNS add-on.\"\n\nRegarding CoreDNS as the default DNS provider, the page explains that CoreDNS serves as the cluster's DNS implementation, describing it as \"a general-purpose authoritative DNS server that can serve as cluster DNS.\" The document treats CoreDNS as the standard implementation being configured rather than explicitly declaring it the default.","durationMs":3041,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/website/main/content/en/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/dns-custom-nameservers.md"}}
